        "thinking": "`ensureSkillInventoryCurrent` calls `ensureBundledSkills` which uses `fs.stat` and reads from the filesystem, and `pruneMissingLocalPathSkills` which also uses the filesystem. So using `companySkillService(db).list()` directly in CF would fail.\n\nFor CF, I need to skip the \"ensure skill inventory\" step (which syncs the DB with the local filesystem) and just query the DB directly. The skills in the DB are already there from previous imports.\n\nSo for the CF skills route, I can just query the `companySkills` table directly without the inventory sync step.\n\n\nI'm mapping out the approach for all five endpoints: adapters and plugins return empty arrays since CF doesn't support them, the skills endpoint queries the database directly without the filesystem sync, join requests just call the database loader, and the user directory needs similar direct database access.\n\nThe challenge with the last two is that they depend on internal helper functions from `access.ts`, so I'm going to write simplified CF-native queries instead that return the same data shape. For user directory, I'm querying active company memberships and joining with user profiles. Join requests is trickier since it involves collapsing duplicates...\n\nWriting database queries...\n\nActually, I should check what the UI actually needs from these endpoints. Looking at the 501 errors—the app mostly works, but the user wants full functionality. For join requests with `?status=pending_approval`, the UI probably just displays a count in the sidebar. Returning an empty list would be functionally correct if there are no pending requests. But for user directory, returning an empty list would incorrectly show no users when there actually are some, so I need to make sure that one returns real data.\n\nI need to query the actual database to get real member and user data. I'm fetching company memberships filtered by company ID, extracting the user IDs, then querying the auth users table to get their details, and finally mapping everything into the response format with principal IDs, status, and user information.\n\nFor the join requests endpoint, I'm trying to understand what the existing helper functions do, but I think the cleaner approach is to create a new CF-native implementation file that queries the same database tables directly. I'll do the same for skills—querying the company skills table and ordering by name and key. But looking at the response shape transformations, this is getting complicated, so I should step back and reconsider the overall strategy here.\n\nRather than converting everything to use the `expressHandler` pattern, I'll register CF-native route handlers directly in `app.ts` before the catch-all, using Hono's routing and the `buildRequestResources` function to access the database. This way I can implement the same logic as the server routes but in a transport-agnostic way. Looking at what each endpoint actually needs—skills requires the company skills and agents tables, join requests needs join requests, invites, and auth users tables, and the user directory needs company memberships and auth users—I can build targeted handlers for each one.\n\nFor the remaining endpoints, some are straightforward stubs like adapters and UI contributions, but others like the WebSocket endpoint, adapter management, and the plugin system aren't feasible in Cloudflare due to their reliance on file system access or Durable Objects for real-time pub/sub. This is really a design decision about which endpoints to support in the worker versus keeping on the Express server.\n\nThe practical approach is to stub out the adapter and plugin endpoints with empty responses, implement the database-only endpoints like skills, join-requests, and user-directory, and handle WebSocket connections natively since Cloudflare Workers support them. For the WebSocket endpoint, I'll set up basic connection handling.\n\nSetting up the extra routes file...",
